Man oh man...
What is going on today?{VAR BE01}
Challengers coming one
right after another.{VAR BE01}
Well, no matter.{VAR BE01}
I am Grimsley of the Elite Four, and I will
fulfill my duty to be your opponent.{VAR BE01}

Whether or not you get to fight at full
strength, whether or not luck smiles{VAR BE00}
on you--none of that matters.{VAR BE01}
Only results matter. And a loss is a loss.{VAR BE01}
See, victory shines like a bright light.{VAR BE01}
And right now, you and your Pokémon
are shining brilliantly.

Now, I'm nothing more than
the guy who lost...{VAR BE01}
Nothing more, nothing less.
Sigh...{VAR BE01}
You should take that strength and test
it against the rest of the Elite Four.

Astonishing!{VAR BE01}
You have defeated every member of
the Pokémon League's Elite Four.{VAR BE01}
But it isn't over yet.{VAR BE01}
There is one more opponent against whom
you must prove your strength.{VAR BE01}
The pinnacle of the Pokémon League,
the Champion, Alder...{VAR BE01}
Or, perhaps, that man called N has
defeated him and taken his title.{VAR BE01}
I don't know which one you will face, but
check the statue in the central plaza,{VAR BE00}
and continue to the final room.

Life is a serious battle, and you have
to use the tools you're given.{VAR BE01}
It's more important to master the cards
you're holding than to complain about{VAR BE00}
the ones your opponents were dealt.{VAR BE01}
Let us begin.
And may the best Trainer win!{VAR BE01}
Contests like this are proof
that you are really living...{VAR BE01}

There are bad ways to win--
and good ways to lose.{VAR BE01}
What's interesting and troubling is that
it's not always clear which is which.{VAR BE01}
A flipped coin doesn't always land
heads or tails.{VAR BE01}
Sometimes it may never land at all...

Excellent!{VAR BE01}
The nice thing about Pokémon battles is
they teach the loser something, as well.{VAR BE01}
That said...
You should take that strength and test{VAR BE00}
it against the rest of the Elite Four!

